The module key did not lack an axis declaration — it carried a false one.
ExternalRef.module_path read:

    # axis: free-text — module import path in source-language grammar;
    #   consumers display/lookup, never branch on the value itself.

A consumer branches on the value itself. io_boundary._module_matches
decides type-vs-sub-package from ORTHOGRAPHY
(longer_raw[shared][:1].isupper()) and carries a Swift carve-out that
branches on the value being a single token the catalogue name ends with.
The static lint accepted the declaration because a free-text
justification must be PRESENT, not TRUE — so the gap survived a check
already reading that exact line. The heuristic it justified is
information-free wherever module names are capitalised: haskell 100%,
swift 97%, objc 95%, elixir 52%, javascript 21%.

AXIOM: the module key names the STATIC OWNER PATH of the called symbol —
the namespace or type in which it is DEFINED — not a property of the call
site. A TYPE IS CONFORMANT, and that is the cut an orthographic heuristic
cannot make: the catalogues are deliberately full of types (net.Conn,
std::fs::File, pathlib.Path) because a method-shaped primitive is
unaddressable without its owning type. A receiver VARIABLE is not — resp
is not where read is defined. ADR-0023's endpoint-property cut reused,
and the tell is that _module_matches already carries a Swift carve-out
whose only purpose is to tolerate the non-conformant shape.

Six notions in four sections. Axiom-conformance is DERIVED from the
section, never stored per-notion: a stored flag beside the section is one
fact in two homes, the shape this axis exists to remove.
pending_classification derives to NOT conformant — "not yet argued", not
"argued and accepted".

The best evidence is four lines of source rather than a percentage.
swift.py:1223 falls back receiver_type -> import alias -> receiver_hint
-> "external": a TYPE, a NAMESPACE, a receiver VARIABLE and a SENTINEL
into one slot with no record of which produced it. That also locates
INV-kotob's swift emission site, which the concept audit failed to find.
Same trace corrected the audit: it recorded objc as writing a receiver
variable, but objc.py:644 is gated on receiver_name[0].isupper() and a
capitalised Objective-C message receiver is normally a CLASS name, so
objc's slot is predominantly a type. The lowercase identifiers the audit
counted came from swift; its "26% of objc+swift refs" is a
combined-cohort figure and is not attributable to objc.

Structural policy, not a value registry — module names are unenumerable,
so this follows qualified_name_axis under ADR-0024 §4's "use judgment"
carveout while the sibling io-boundary axis takes the heavyweight shape.
Citations are checked rather than trusted: each notion cites its producer
site by file:line plus an anchor, and a test asserts the line still
contains it.

DEVIATIONS, STATED: nothing an analyzer emits changes (external_symbol
ids embed the slot, so migration needs the v9/v10 scheme bump WI-talos
gates — ADR-0024 step 7, WI-marok), and no drift linter ships, because
the AST walker checks a consumer set against a registry and there is no
enumerable value set to check. The # axis: declaration lint is the live
enforcement and is what the ir.py change satisfies.

Two open questions recorded, neither answered: global_object
(process/window/navigator) is genuinely contested and left for a
per-value audit with no row moved; ExternalRef.name carries the SAME
"never branch on the value itself" clause while
strip_redundant_module_qualifier decomposes it with rpartition(".") —
filed as WI-biguz rather than folded in.

19,742 tests pass, 100% coverage, mypy ratchet clean.

